    {
      "id": "silent-letters",
      "title": "Silent letters",
      "fr": "les lettres muettes",
      "level": "A1",
      "keywords": [
        "silent letters",
        "pronunciation",
        "final consonants",
        "-e",
        "-ent",
        "CaReFuL",
        "h",
        "lettres muettes"
      ],
      "topics": [],
      "see": [
        "liaison",
        "conjugation-basics",
        "plural",
        "accents"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "In short",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "en": "French writes many more letters than it pronounces. Most often silent are **final consonants** and the final **-e**."
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "head": [
                "what",
                "example",
                "sounds roughly like"
              ],
              "textCols": [
                0,
                2
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "final -s, -t, -d, -x, -z",
                  "petit, grand, deux, nez",
                  "peti, gran, dö, ne"
                ],
                [
                  "final -e",
                  "table, porte",
                  "tabl, port"
                ],
                [
                  "-ent in verbs",
                  "ils parlent",
                  "il parl"
                ],
                [
                  "-er, -ez at the end",
                  "parler, parlez",
                  "parle"
                ],
                [
                  "h",
                  "hôtel, homme",
                  "otel, om"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "CaReFuL",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "en": "The final letters *c, r, f, l*, on the other hand, are mostly **pronounced**: *sac, bonjour, neuf, avril*. The mnemonic: the English word **CaReFuL**. Exception: *-er* at the end of verbs and many other words (*parler, premier*) is read as *-é*."
            },
            {
              "type": "tip",
              "en": "*-ent* is silent only in verbs (*ils parlent*). In other words it is read as a nasal: *souvent, le vent, content*."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Why it matters",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "en": "What you don't hear you have to watch for in writing: verb endings (*il parle, ils parlent*), adjective agreement (*petit, petits*) and the plural. In dictation these are the most common mistakes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Examples",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "examples",
              "items": [
                {
                  "fr": "Il est petit.",
                  "en": "He is small."
                },
                {
                  "fr": "Ils jouent au foot.",
                  "en": "They play football."
                },
                {
                  "fr": "J'ai un sac noir.",
                  "en": "I have a black bag."
                },
                {
                  "fr": "Il fait froid en hiver.",
                  "en": "It's cold in winter."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "checks": [
        {
          "id": "g-silent-letters-01",
          "type": "cloze",
          "fr": "Ils ___ au foot.",
          "a": [
            "jouent"
